# Render components as elements

A function whose name starts with a capital letter and returns React nodes is a component. Render
it as an element:

```tsx
<ListEpisodeNodes channel={channel} items={items} viewSelected={viewSelected} />
```

A direct call (`ListEpisodeNodes({ channel, items, viewSelected })`) runs that function inside the
parent. Its `useState`, `useMemo`, and other hooks belong to the parent. An early `return` above
the call, or any branch that skips it, changes how many hooks the parent runs. React then throws
a change in the order of Hooks.

Hooks that belong to the parent stay above every `return`. A child that mounts only on some
renders is an element, so its hooks start and stop with that element.

A camelCase helper that returns nodes (`renderNodes`, `renderInlineNodes`) is a plain function.
Keep calling it. Do not give that helper hooks.
